# Twilight Syndrome: Kyuumei-hen (SLPS-00405): 16-bit little-endian codes (01 00 = あ), keys in memory byte order.
# This file is what every chapter shares: 0x0000 is a space and 0x0001-0x00DB the kana, digits and punctuation.
# Kanji and Latin letters are 0x00DC up and numbered anew by each chapter: one of SLPS-00405-ch5.tbl, -ch6, -ch7,
# -ch8, -ch9, -chA or -chH goes after this file, chosen by the byte at 0x80016635 (0-6 in that order).
# Read from the game's own drawing: a replay made it draw every code, and each glyph was read by eye.
# Not mapped: 0x00CA, 0x00CB (marks not made out), 0x00D6 (a page icon), 0x00D8-0x00DB (ornate A B C D); no
# scene's text uses them. ° (0x00C5) stands in for an apostrophe the one time it is used.
# Words from 0x8000 up are commands: 0x8100 new line, 0x8500 wait for a press with the text going on below
# (the window scrolls), 0x8600 wait for a press at a page's end (the window is cleared), 0x8D0n a speaker's page
# (a name of n codes follows, then ：), 0x84nn indent, 0xFFFF the script's end; the rest (high bytes 0x82-0x8B,
# 0x97, 0x98, 0xA7, 0xA8, 0xC2-0xD9) drive answers, pauses and pictures. 288 different ones are used. Some seem
# to take a word below 0x8000 as an argument (0x0002 after 0xCD00 and 0xCD80, 0x007F after 0xC804).
# The running script is at 0x801FE800, and the u16 at 0x800A80E2 is the index of the next word the game will read
# (just past the wait code while it waits for a press). Seen in chapter 6's first scene only.
